(window.LADDER_CARDS["39a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39a1.1","q":"What does the and-as-product technique say, and why does x² = x hold on {0, 1} and not off it? How do cos 0 and cos(π/2) read the same two numbers?","a":"For x and y in {0, 1} the mark of “both” is the product xy; 0 · 0 = 0 and 1 · 1 = 1, while 2 · 2 = 4; cos 0 = 1 is the full share of a direction in itself and cos(π/2) = 0 is no share at all, the right angle that carries the mark of the totally false","w":["The mark of “both” is the sum x + y; x² = x holds for every number; cos 0 = 0 and cos(π/2) = 1","The mark of “both” is the product xy; x² = x holds because squaring always shrinks a number; cos 0 and cos(π/2) are both 1","The mark of “both” is the greater of x and y; x² = x fails at 1; the circle has no reading of 0 and 1"],"why":"The arithmetic of the step lives on the two numbers only."},
 {"u":"39a2.1","q":"What does the or-as-greater technique say? Why does x + y fall short of the mark of “at least one” on exactly one row?","a":"For x and y in {0, 1} the mark of “at least one” is the greater of x and y, which is x + y − xy, and the mark of “not x” is 1 − x; x + y counts how many are true, so on the row 11 it is 2, and the overlap xy must be taken off once","w":["The mark of “at least one” is x + y and of “not x” is −x; the sum is wrong on the row 00","The mark of “at least one” is xy; the sum is wrong on the rows 01 and 10, where it gives 1","The mark of “at least one” is the lesser of x and y; the sum is right on every row"],"why":"Only the row where both are true counts something twice."},
 {"u":"39a3.1","q":"What does the remainder-by-two technique say? Why is the twos place of x + y the and of x and y?","a":"For x and y in {0, 1} the mark of “exactly one” is the remainder of x + y by 2, which is x + y − 2xy; x + y = 2 · xy + (x + y − 2xy), and the sum reaches 2 only when both are 1, which is the row where xy = 1","w":["The mark of “exactly one” is x + y − xy; the twos place is the or, since a carry comes whenever one of them is 1","The mark of “exactly one” is the remainder of xy by 2; the twos place is x ⊕ y","The mark of “exactly one” is x + y; there is no twos place, since marks are 0 or 1"],"why":"The first line of the proof is an adder: twice the and, plus the exactly-one."},
 {"u":"39a4.1","q":"Why is x ∨ y not x + y? Why does x² = x not give x = 1?","a":"On the row 11 the sum is 2, which is no mark, so the overlap xy must be taken off; and x = 0 meets x² = x too, since dividing by x needs x ≠ 0","w":["On the row 00 the sum is 0, which is no mark; and x² = x gives x = 1 because 0 is not a number","The sum is always one too large; and x² = x has no answers on {0, 1}","x ∨ y is x + y on every row; and x² = x gives x = 1 by dividing both sides by x"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39b1.1","q":"What is a trace, and what do V and f mean in V cos(2πft)? Why does one recipe serve a wave at every frequency and every start?","a":"A wire’s voltage as a function of time, one number v(t) at each moment; V is the size of the turning and f its frequency, in turns a second; sliding a wave later multiplies its turnings’ coefficients by a turning, and stretching time keeps the coefficients (rung 32)","w":["A picture of a wire; V is the frequency and f the size; each frequency needs a recipe of its own","A wire’s voltage as a function of time; V is the size and f the frequency; a recipe serves every wave because all waves have the same coefficients","A list of samples; V is the start and f the stop; sliding a wave changes which turnings it has"],"why":"A trace is a function, so what rung 32 built for functions applies to it."},
 {"u":"39b2.1","q":"What does the sign-wave technique say? Where do the ½ and the alternating signs come from?","a":"The sign of cos θ, read as 1 or 0, is ½ + (2/π)(cos θ − cos 3θ/3 + cos 5θ/5 − ⋯); the ½ is the wave’s average, on for half the time, and the signs are sin(nπ/2) = 1, −1, 1, … for odd n, from sliding rung 32’s square a quarter turn","w":["It is (4/π)(sin θ + sin 3θ/3 + ⋯); there is no ½, and every sign is plus","It is ½ + (2/π)(cos θ + cos 2θ/2 + cos 3θ/3 + ⋯); the ½ is the first turning and the signs come from the even speeds","It is ½ + (2/π) cos θ and nothing more; the ½ is the threshold"],"why":"Nothing in the proof is new except the reading: halve the slid square and add ½."},
 {"u":"39b3.1","q":"What does the first-turning technique say, and which line of its proof needs 2/π to be above 0? What do the higher turnings add, if not the bits?","a":"Read against the threshold ½, the first turning u = ½ + (2/π) cos θ alone gives back every 1 and 0 of the sign wave; the line from (2/π) cos θ > 0 to cos θ > 0, since multiplying by a positive number keeps the order; the higher turnings sharpen the edges, saying more exactly when a change came","w":["The first turning is itself a square wave; no line needs 2/π above 0; the higher turnings carry half the bits","Read against the threshold ½ the first turning gives back every bit; the line that takes ½ from both sides; the higher turnings raise the wave’s average","The first turning loses every second bit; the last line; the higher turnings carry the lost bits"],"why":"The shape of a wave and the message on it are different things."},
 {"u":"39b4.1","q":"What does the threshold-reading technique say? Why does one more spread of margin cut the misreads by far more than one more spread’s worth?","a":"A level with margin d under bell noise of spread σ is misread with the chance that the bell lies more than d/σ spreads to one side; the bell’s tail falls far faster than in proportion: P(Z > 6) ≈ 9.9 · 10⁻¹⁰ and P(Z > 7) ≈ 1.3 · 10⁻¹², a factor of nearly 800","w":["A level is misread with chance σ/d; one more spread cuts the misreads by a seventh","A level with margin d is never misread once d is above σ; more margin changes nothing","A level is misread with the chance that the bell lies more than d/σ spreads to one side; the tail falls in proportion, so 7 spreads give 6/7 of the misreads"],"why":"The reading is wrong at a rate, and the rate is known."},
 {"u":"39b5.1","q":"Why is u = ½ + (2/π) cos θ not a square wave, though its reading is? Why is twice the margin not half the misreads?","a":"u is a smooth turning; only its reading against ½ is the square wave’s; and the bell’s tail falls far faster than in proportion: about 10⁻³³ at 12 spreads against 10⁻⁹ at 6","w":["u is a square wave with rounded corners; and twice the margin is exactly half the misreads","u is a smooth turning whose reading is another smooth turning; and twice the margin is a quarter of the misreads","u has jumps at the threshold; and the misreads do not depend on the margin"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39c1.1","q":"What does the power-by-frequency technique say, and which technique of rung 32 gives it? Why is the average square of the sign wave half of V²?","a":"The average power a wave delivers to a resistance is the sum, frequency by frequency, of the average powers its turnings would deliver alone; 32b3’s energy-kept technique; s² = s since s is 0 or 1, and the wave is on for half of each turn, so the average of v² is ½V²","w":["The average power is the square of the average voltage; 32h1’s square recipe; the average square is ¼V², the square of ½V","The average power is the power of the largest turning alone; 32g1’s samples; the average square is V², since the wave reaches V","The average power is the sum over frequencies; 32b3’s energy-kept technique; the average square is ½V² because cos² averages ½"],"why":"The parts add to the whole: ¼V² steady, about 0.203V² in the first turning, about 0.047V² in the rest."},
 {"u":"39c2.1","q":"What does the known-wave technique say? Why does a wall socket deliver kilowatts and no bits, and a coin-driven square wave a bit at each change?","a":"A wave fixed by numbers the reader already has tells the reader nothing, 0 bits whatever its power; the socket’s size, frequency and start are known, so only one pattern is possible, and log₂ 1 = 0; the coin’s wave chooses between two at each moment it may change, log₂ 2 = 1 bit","w":["A wave of one frequency carries one bit a turn; the socket carries 60 bits a second; the coin’s wave carries none because it is random","A wave tells as many bits as it has watts; the socket carries the most; the coin’s wave carries a bit only if its power is high","A known wave tells 0 bits; the socket has no bits because its voltage is too high to read; the coin’s wave has a bit because it is square"],"why":"A message lives in what the reader could not have predicted."},
 {"u":"39c3.1","q":"What does the changes-per-second technique say? Which two techniques of 32g give the “at most” and the “free”?","a":"A wire that passes frequencies up to B carries at most 2B free values a second, and with M levels at each, at most 2B log₂ M bits a second; 32g1’s samples-are-coefficients technique gives the at most, since the samples at spacing 1/(2B) fix the signal, and 32g2’s sample-rebuild technique gives the free, since any list of samples gives such a signal","w":["A wire carries B values a second and B log₂ M bits; 32g2 gives the at most and 32g1 the free","A wire carries at most 2B free values a second and 2BM bits; both come from 32g1","A wire carries as many values a second as its power allows; neither technique of 32g is used"],"why":"M^(2B) patterns a second, and the log of a power."},
 {"u":"39c4.1","q":"Why do the levels a reader can tell apart grow like √(1 + S/N) and not like 1 + S/N? Why does doubling the power buy only about half a bit more per change, while doubling B doubles the bits?","a":"Levels are told apart by the size of the signal against the size of the noise, and size goes as the square root of power; each change carries about ½ log₂(1 + S/N) bits, so once S/N is large doubling S adds ½ log₂ 2 = ½ bit a change, B bits a second, while the bits B log₂(1 + S/N) are in proportion to B","w":["Levels go by power, so they grow like 1 + S/N; doubling the power doubles the bits, and doubling B adds one bit","Levels go by the size of the signal against the noise; doubling the power adds a whole bit per change; doubling B adds half a bit","The levels do not depend on the noise; doubling the power or B each doubles the bits"],"why":"The capacity is used here; its proof is held in trust."},
 {"u":"39c5.1","q":"Why is more power not more message? Why is the average square of a wave not the square of its average?","a":"The socket delivers kilowatts and 0 bits, since a message is about the patterns that could have come; and for the sign wave the average square is ½V² against ¼V² for the square of the average: the swinging turnings carry power the average misses","w":["More power is always more message, by the capacity; and the two averages are equal for every wave","The socket carries no message because its power is too low; and the square of the average is the larger of the two","A message needs no power at all; and the average square is always twice the square of the average"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39d1.1","q":"What does the reverse-leak technique say, and which fact about exp does its first line use? Why is a diode a switch that nobody works?","a":"Pushed backwards, a diode passes less than Iₛ, however hard it is pushed; exp is above 0 everywhere and rises, with exp 0 = 1, so V < 0 gives 0 < e^(V/Vₜ) < 1; its state is set by the voltages around it, closed one way and open the other, with no wire to steer it","w":["Pushed backwards a diode passes nothing at all; exp of a negative number is negative; a diode is worked by its gate","Pushed backwards a diode passes less than Iₛ; exp falls, with exp 0 = 0; a diode is a switch worked by a third wire","Pushed backwards a diode passes as much as forwards; exp is its own slope; a diode is a resistor, not a switch"],"why":"That is what makes it a valve and not yet a gate that can be steered."},
 {"u":"39d2.1","q":"What does the diode-gate technique say? Why is the either gate’s output one diode drop below the higher input?","a":"The either gate’s output sits one diode drop below the higher input and the both gate’s one drop above the lower input, so read against a threshold they are the greater and the lesser; the higher input’s diode is forward and must carry the resistor’s current, which takes about 0.57 V across it, while the other diode is pushed backwards and passes almost nothing","w":["Both gates give exactly the higher input; there is no drop, since a forward diode is a closed wire","The either gate sits one drop above the higher input and the both gate one drop below the lower; the drop comes from the resistor","The either gate gives the sum of its inputs less a drop; the drop is the leak of the backward diode"],"why":"5 V in gives about 4.43 V out: the gates work, and they spend level."},
 {"u":"39d3.1","q":"What does the rising-network technique say? Which line of its proof uses that each part’s current rises with the voltage across it, and why does that forbid a not?","a":"In a network of diodes and resistors, raising inputs and lowering none never lowers the voltage of any node; the line that says the current from the node that fell most to each neighbour does not rise, since the voltage across each part does not rise; a not would have to send its output down when its input goes up","w":["Raising an input lowers some node; the line that takes the largest fall; a not is allowed, since some node falls","Raising inputs never lowers a node; the line that cites the current law; it forbids an either, since an either must fall","Raising inputs raises every node by the same amount; the last line; it forbids nothing"],"why":"Valves make both and either and nothing that turns a 1 into a 0."},
 {"u":"39d4.1","q":"What does the no-gain technique say? Why would restoring a weak level need a slope above 1 near the threshold?","a":"In a network of diodes and resistors, if no input rises by more than Δ, no node rises by more than Δ; a gate that restored a weak level would have to move its output further than its input moved, and valves never do, so every stage spends margin: 5 V falls to 4.43, 3.86, 3.29, 2.73 V","w":["If no input rises by more than Δ, some node rises by more; restoring needs a slope below 1, which valves have","No node rises by more than Δ; restoring needs a slope of exactly 1, which a resistor gives","Every node rises by exactly Δ; restoring needs no slope at all, only a larger resistor"],"why":"The cure is a part with its own supply, steered by the signal."},
 {"u":"39d5.1","q":"Why is no cleverer network of diodes and resistors a not? Why does a resistor not add the lost 0.6 V back?","a":"The rising-network technique covers every network of diodes and resistors, not one design; and by the no-gain technique a resistor alone moves no node further than an input moved: adding back needs a supply steered by the signal","w":["Only the networks tried so far fail; and a large enough resistor does add the 0.6 V back","The rising-network technique covers only two-input networks; and a resistor adds voltage but not current","A not can be made from three diodes; and the lost 0.6 V is not lost at all"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39e"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39e"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39e1.1","q":"What does the pair-inverter technique say? Why does it escape the rising-network technique of 39d3?","a":"A switch that closes on high from the output to ground, and one that closes on low from the supply to the output, with their gates joined, make a not: the output is 1 − x; the rising-network technique was about parts whose state is set by the voltages on their own ends, and here the state is set by a third wire, the gate, so raising it cuts the path to the supply","w":["Two switches in a row make a both; it does not escape, since switches are valves","The pair makes a not; it escapes because switches pass current both ways","The pair makes a not; it escapes because the supply voltage rises when the gate rises"],"why":"A not is a fall, and only a steered part can fall."},
 {"u":"39e2.1","q":"What does the middle-gain technique say, and why is G′(m) the square of g′(m)? Why does a slope of −3.3 at the middle make the end rests hold so hard?","a":"The middle rest of two not gates in a row holds when the slope of g there is below 1 in size and pushes every nearby voltage away when it is above 1; by the chain rule G′(m) = g′(g(m)) g′(m), and g(m) = m; a curve steep in the middle is flat at its ends, where G′ is about 0.0003, so a level near a rail is pulled in: 1.2 V goes to 0.029 and then 0.005","w":["The middle rest always holds; G′(m) = 2g′(m) by the sum rule; a steep middle makes the ends steep too","The middle rest pushes when the slope is below 1; G′(m) = g′(m)² by the product rule; the ends hold because the supply is fixed","The middle rest holds when the slope is above 1; G′(m) = g′(m); the ends do not hold"],"why":"With g′(m) = −3.3, G′(m) is 10.9: the middle pushes and the ends pull."},
 {"u":"39e3.1","q":"What does the noise-floor technique say? Why does the same argument give a ceiling that grows with n for a chain of valves?","a":"If each pair of stages adds noise at most η and the pair’s slope near a rail is at most k with k below 1, the error stays below η/(1 − k) at every stage; the error obeys eₙ₊₁ ≤ k eₙ + η, so eₙ ≤ η(1 + k + ⋯ + kⁿ⁻¹), and for valves the slope is at most 1, so with k = 1 the sum is ηn","w":["The error is at most ηn for every chain; valves do better because their slope is 1","The error stays below η/(1 − k); for valves k is 0, so the ceiling is η","The error doubles at each stage; for valves it halves"],"why":"A gate with a steep middle has a flat end, and the flat end forgets."},
 {"u":"39e4.1","q":"What does the steering technique say? Which of 39c’s two questions does the gate answer, and which does the strip?","a":"A switch worked by a voltage spends on its gate about the gate charge times the gate voltage at each change, while the power it steers is set by its supply and load, and their ratio is its power gain; the gate carries the message, when to switch, and the strip carries the power: 33 microwatts steer 24 watts","w":["The gate spends as much power as the load; the gate carries the power and the strip the message","The gate spends charge times voltage at each change; the gate and the strip both carry the message","The switch makes the power it steers; the gate answers neither question"],"why":"The transistor copies the pattern of the gate onto the power of the strip."},
 {"u":"39e5.1","q":"Why do the end rests hold though the middle pushes? Where do the 24 W come from, if not the gate?","a":"The end rests have slope near 0.0003, far below 1, so they hold; only the middle, with slope above 1, pushes; and the 24 W come from the pump’s supply, which the gate only steers","w":["Every rest of G pushes, the ends only more slowly; and the 24 W come from the gate’s charge","The end rests hold because the middle pushes toward them with slope 1; and the gain of 7 · 10⁵ makes the energy","The end rests have slope 10.9; and the 24 W come from the noise"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39f"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39f"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39f1.1","q":"What does the nand-alone technique say? Which line uses x² = x, and why would it fail off {0, 1}?","a":"Not x is the not-and of x with itself, x and y is the not of their not-and, and x or y is the not-and of their nots; the line nand(x, x) = 1 − x² = 1 − x; off {0, 1} x² is not x, as 2 · 2 = 4","w":["Not-and is built from not and and; the line 1 − (1 − xy) = xy uses x² = x; it holds for every number","Not x is nand(x, x), and is the not of the not-and, or is the not-and of the nots; the line for or uses x² = x; it fails off {0, 1} because 1 − x is negative","Not x is nand(x, 0); no line uses x² = x"],"why":"One gate for not, two for both, three for either."},
 {"u":"39f2.1","q":"What does the split-on-one technique say? Why does the count of gates double with each input?","a":"Every table of n inputs is (x ∧ f₁) ∨ (¬x ∧ f₀), and a circuit of not-ands for it takes at most 5 · 2ⁿ⁻¹ − 4 gates; the recipe builds both halves f₁ and f₀, each a table of n − 1 inputs, and spends 4 more: G(n) ≤ 2G(n − 1) + 4","w":["Every table is x ∧ f₁, and takes at most n gates; each input adds one gate","Every table is (x ∧ f₁) ∨ (¬x ∧ f₀), with at most 5n − 4 gates; the count grows by 5 with each input","Every table is the either of one both for each row; the count squares with each input"],"why":"1 gate for one input, at most 6 for two, 16 for three, 76 for five."},
 {"u":"39f3.1","q":"What does the most-tables-hard technique say? Why does it prove that a hard table exists without naming one?","a":"If (n + 2 + g)^(2g + 1) is less than 2^(2ⁿ), some table of n inputs needs more than g not-ands; it counts: there are fewer circuits of g gates than tables, and each circuit gives one table, so some table has no such circuit, whichever it is","w":["If (n + 2 + g)^(2g + 1) is more than 2^(2ⁿ), every table needs g gates; it names the table of all 1s","Some table of n inputs needs more than g gates when the count of circuits is below the count of tables; it names the hard table by running every circuit","Every table needs exactly 2ⁿ gates; it proves it by building each one"],"why":"At 10 inputs, 2¹⁰¹⁹ is below 2¹⁰²⁴, so some table needs more than 78 gates."},
 {"u":"39f4.1","q":"Why do both and either alone not build every table? Why is the split-on-one count not a lower count?","a":"Both and either only rise (39d3), so no table that falls, such as not, is reached; and 5 · 2ⁿ⁻¹ − 4 is an upper count for one recipe: it says that many gates are enough, not that some table needs them","w":["Both and either build every table but slowly; and the split-on-one count is both an upper and a lower count","Both and either miss only the tables of three inputs; and the count is a lower count for the recipe","Both and either only fall; and the count says every table needs that many gates"],"why":"The lower count is the most-tables-hard technique’s."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39g"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39g"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39g1.1","q":"What does the delay-loop technique say? Why is the ½ that meets x = 1 − x the middle voltage of 39e2, and why does it not hold?","a":"A not whose output feeds its own input has no mark that rests, and with a delay τ its output is 1 and 0 in turn, each for τ, with period 2τ; on a wire ½ is the point m where the gate’s curve gives back its own input, g(m) = m; 39e2 proved that rest does not hold when the slope there is steep","w":["The loop rests at 1; ½ is the threshold of 39b4, and it holds because noise is small","The loop has no mark that rests and period τ; ½ is the supply voltage; it does not hold because the gate has no delay","The loop rests at ½ for ever; ½ is the middle voltage, and it holds because the slope is steep"],"why":"Read once, the liar has no mark; read over time, it has a steady pattern."},
 {"u":"39g2.1","q":"What does the odd-ring technique say? Why does an odd number of falling gates make a falling ring, and why does that leave exactly one rest?","a":"A ring of an odd number of not gates has exactly one rest, the middle voltage m, and when |g′(m)| > 1 it does not hold; a fall after a fall is a rise, so an odd count of falls is a fall; then h(v) − v falls strictly from above 0 to below 0, so it is 0 at exactly one v, by in-between values and strict falling","w":["An odd ring has three rests; an odd count of falls is a rise; a rising h crosses the line three times","An odd ring has exactly one rest, and it holds; falls cancel in pairs leaving none; one rest because h is constant","An odd ring has no rest at all; h falls, so it never meets the line v"],"why":"So an odd ring cannot sit still."},
 {"u":"39g3.1","q":"What does the ring-period technique say? Why does the change go round the ring twice in each period?","a":"A ring of an odd number n of not gates, each with delay τ, runs with period 2nτ, and a ring of an even number repeats any pattern every nτ and may rest; once round, n nots and n delays leave one not, x₁(t) = 1 − x₁(t − nτ), so one trip turns x₁ from 1 to 0 and a second turns it back","w":["An odd ring has period nτ; the change goes round once","An odd ring has period 2nτ; the change goes round twice because each gate has two switches","An odd ring has period 2τ whatever n is; the change never goes round"],"why":"For n = 101 and τ = 15 ps the period is 3030 ps, about 330 MHz."},
 {"u":"39g4.1","q":"Why is the liar box not stuck at ½? Why is the period 2nτ and not nτ?","a":"½ is its one rest and that rest does not hold, so it runs; and for n odd the change must go round twice, once to flip x₁ and once to flip it back","w":["It is stuck at ½, which is why it has no mark; and the period is nτ","½ is not a rest at all; and the period is 2nτ because each gate delays twice","It is stuck at ½ until the power goes off; and the period is 2nτ only for even rings"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39h"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39h"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39h1.1","q":"What does the even-ring technique say? Why does the ring’s state depend on its history and not on its logic?","a":"A ring of an even number of not gates with |g′(m)| > 1 rests at the middle and at two patterns of 1s and 0s; the middle pushes and the two patterns hold; its logic is x = x, which both marks meet, so nothing in it picks one: when the power comes on noise tips the ring off m and it stays at the end it fell to","w":["An even ring has no rest and runs as a clock; its state is set by x = 1 − x","An even ring rests only at the middle, which holds; its state is set by its logic, since x = x has one answer","An even ring rests at two patterns and the middle, and all three hold; its state is set by the supply voltage"],"why":"To use the ring, it must be told which end."},
 {"u":"39h2.1","q":"What does the hold-row technique say? Why is the row s = r = 0 kept out?","a":"With both doors at 1 the latch is an even ring and keeps its bit; s = 0 sets Q to 1 and r = 0 clears Q to 0; with both at 0, Q = P = 1, which is no bit, and when both doors are released together the ring races and tips whichever way noise says","w":["With both doors at 0 the latch keeps its bit; the row s = r = 1 is kept out because it clears and sets at once","With both doors at 1 the latch keeps its bit; s = r = 0 is kept out because it burns the gates","s = 0 clears Q and r = 0 sets it; the row s = r = 0 holds the bit"],"why":"Set, then hold: the moment of the 0 has been remembered."},
 {"u":"39h3.1","q":"What does the edge-catch technique say? Why can changes of d while the clock is 1 not reach the output?","a":"Two gated latches in a row, the first enabled by not c and the second by c, take the value of d at the moment c rises and hold it until the next rise; while c is 1 the first latch is shut, so changes of d reach only a shut latch, and the second follows the first, which no longer moves","w":["One gated latch takes d at the rise; while c is 1 the output follows d","Two latches in a row take d at the rise; while c is 1 changes of d cannot pass because the second latch is shut","Two latches in a row take d at the fall of the clock; while c is 1 both are open"],"why":"The output changes only at a rise, to d as it was then."},
 {"u":"39h4.1","q":"What does the register-count technique say? What would a row of catches fed its own value plus 1 do at each rise?","a":"A row of n edge-catches on one clock holds any one of 2ⁿ patterns and takes a new one only at a rise: 64 catches hold any number below 2⁶⁴; fed its own value plus 1, it would take the next number at each rise, so it counts","w":["A row of n catches holds n patterns; fed its own value plus 1 it would hold still","A row of n catches holds 2ⁿ patterns and changes whenever its input does; fed its own value plus 1 it would run away between rises","A row of n catches holds n² patterns; fed its own value plus 1 it would double at each rise"],"why":"Two states each, held independently: the product rule."},
 {"u":"39h5.1","q":"Why does x = x not pick the bit? Why does one gated latch not catch d at an instant?","a":"Both marks meet x = x, so the history picks; and a gated latch follows d for the whole time its enable is 1: it takes two in a row to catch an instant","w":["x = x has no answer on {0, 1}; and a gated latch catches d at the moment e rises","x = x picks 1, the truth; and a gated latch never follows d","Only ½ meets x = x; and one gated latch catches an instant if the clock is fast"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39i"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39i"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39i1.1","q":"What does the three-bit-sum technique say? Why are xy and (x ⊕ y)c never both 1?","a":"x + y + c is twice “at least two of x, y, c”, plus x ⊕ y ⊕ c; if xy = 1 then x and y are both 1, so x ⊕ y = 0 and (x ⊕ y)c = 0, which lets their sum be written as an either with no overlap","w":["x + y + c is twice x ∨ y ∨ c plus xyc; they are never both 1 because c is 0","x + y + c is twice “at least two”, plus x ⊕ y ⊕ c; they are never both 1 because a carry in rules out a carry made","x + y + c is “at least two” plus twice x ⊕ y ⊕ c; they can both be 1, on the row 111"],"why":"The carry out is make, or pass and carry in; the sum bit is pass ⊕ carry in."},
 {"u":"39i2.1","q":"What does the carry-delay technique say? Which pair of numbers makes the carry wait longest, and why?","a":"In a chain of n places the last carry may wait 2nτ, and it does when every place passes and none makes; x = 0111…1 and y = 0…01: the carry made in place 0 must pass through every place above it, two delays each","w":["The last carry waits 2τ whatever n is; the pair 0 + 0 waits longest","The last carry may wait 2nτ; the pair 111…1 + 111…1 waits longest, since every place makes a carry","The last carry may wait nτ²; every pair waits the same"],"why":"For 64 places at 15 ps that is 1920 ps, which caps the clock."},
 {"u":"39i3.1","q":"What does the carry-tree technique say, and why does it need the grouping not to matter? Why does the tree wait less though it uses more gates?","a":"The combining of (make, pass) pairs is grouping-free, so every carry of an n-place adder is found in about log₂ n levels of combining, each two gates deep; only then does any tree of pairings give the same block pair as the chain; the tree does about n log₂ n combinings in all, but no path is longer than about log₂ n of them","w":["The combining turns about, so the places can be taken in any order; the tree waits less because it uses fewer gates","The combining is grouping-free, giving log₂ n levels; the grouping is needed so that carries can be dropped; the tree waits less because its gates are faster","Every carry is found in one level; grouping does not come into it"],"why":"Spending more gates to wait less is the trade every fast circuit makes."},
 {"u":"39i4.1","q":"What does the twos-complement technique say? Why does 127 + 1 read −128 in a row of 8 places, and how can a machine see that it happened?","a":"In a row of n places −x is written as 2ⁿ − x, every bit of x turned over plus 1, so x − y is x plus that with the top carry dropped; 127 + 1 = 10000000, and a top bit of 1 marks a negative, 128 − 256 = −128, because 128 is out of range; the wrap happens exactly when two numbers of the same sign give a sum of the other sign","w":["−x is every bit of x turned over; 127 + 1 reads −128 because the adder is broken; it cannot be seen","−x is 2ⁿ − x; 127 + 1 reads 128 in 8 places; a machine sees a wrap when the top carry is dropped","−x is x with its top bit set; 127 + 1 reads −127; a machine sees it when the sum is 0"],"why":"The wrap is the price of a fixed row."},
 {"u":"39i5.1","q":"Why is the carry not x ∨ y ∨ c? Why is −9 not 11110110 in 8 places?","a":"x ∨ y ∨ c is “at least one”, and the carry is “at least two”; and 11110110 is every bit of 9 turned over, 246: add 1 to get 247 = 256 − 9","w":["The carry is “all three”; and −9 is 10001001, 9 with a sign bit","The carry is x ∨ y ∨ c whenever c is 1; and 11110110 is −9 exactly","The carry is x ⊕ y ⊕ c; and −9 cannot be written in 8 places"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39j"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39j"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39j1.1","q":"What does the instruction-number technique say? What makes 290 an instruction and not data?","a":"A number of three digits, first digit d and last two digits n, is read as an instruction: d says what to do and n says which cell, so 290 is “A takes A plus cell 90”; nothing in the cell marks it: it is read as an instruction only when the counter points at it","w":["Every number above 100 is an instruction; 290 is an instruction because it is even","The first two digits say which cell and the last what to do; 290 is an instruction because it sits in cell 90","A number is an instruction when a mark beside it says so; 290 has the mark"],"why":"One number, one instruction."},
 {"u":"39j2.1","q":"What does the fetch-run technique say? Why is each turn a function, and what does that make of a whole run?","a":"The machine’s whole state, its counter, accumulator and memory, is one list of numbers, and each turn is a function from states to states, so a run is fixed by its first state; gates compute only tables of their inputs and every catch takes its input at the rise, so one state gives one next state; a run is a start and a step, the recursion of 4d","w":["Each turn depends on the state and on chance; a run is fixed only on average","The state is the counter alone; each turn is a function because the counter adds 1; a run is fixed by its last state","Each turn is a function of the program only, not of the memory; so every run of one program is the same whatever its data"],"why":"Same start, same run."},
 {"u":"39j3.1","q":"How many turns does the program take for a count k, and where do the 9 and the 3 come from? Why does the program need cell 93 to hold 1?","a":"9k + 3: nine instructions, cells 0 to 8, run in each of the k rounds, and the last test, jump and stop take three more; the machine takes away only the number in a cell, so the 1 it takes from the count must sit in a cell","w":["9k: nine instructions a round and nothing else; cell 93 holds the answer","k + 9: one turn a round and nine to stop; cell 93 holds 1 so that the product starts at 1","9k + 3: nine instructions a round and three to load x; cell 93 holds 1 to mark the end of the program"],"why":"For x = 3 and k = 4 it leaves 12 in cell 92 after 39 turns."},
 {"u":"39j4.1","q":"What does the stored-list technique say? Why can the programs of this machine be listed?","a":"Since instructions are numbers in the cells data is stored in, one machine runs any program placed in its memory, and a program can change the instructions it will run next; every program is a finite list of numbers, and such lists can be listed, as 35a listed the lists","w":["Instructions are kept apart from data, so a program cannot change; programs cannot be listed, since there are uncountably many","One machine runs any program in its memory; programs can be listed because there are only finitely many","A program can change its data but never its instructions; programs can be listed because each is one number below 1000"],"why":"A store of 291 into cell 3 turns “add cell 90” into “add cell 91”."},
 {"u":"39j5.1","q":"Why must the counter move on before the instruction runs, for the jump to work? Why is a processor no more than a table applied again and again?","a":"A jump writes its cell number into the counter; if the counter moved on after running, it would add 1 to that and land one cell late; and each turn is a function of the bits the machine holds, a table, however big","w":["The counter must move on first so that the jump can be skipped; and a processor is a table because it has ten digits","The order does not matter; and a processor is more than a table because it can change its program","The counter moves on after running; and a processor is a table only when it has no memory"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39k"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39k"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39k1.1","q":"What does the loop-count technique say? Why is the count taken over the worst input of each size?","a":"A loop whose body takes b turns and runs r times, with s turns outside it, takes br + s turns, and a loop inside a loop with inner rounds r₁, r₂, … takes b(r₁ + r₂ + ⋯) plus the outer loop’s own turns; “takes” means at most, the count that holds for every input of size n, since a search that finds its match first is quick only by luck","w":["A loop takes b + r + s turns; the count is taken over the best input, since that is what a good program reaches","A loop takes br + s turns; the count is taken over the average input, since the worst never comes","Loops inside loops add; the count is taken over one input chosen at random"],"why":"The search through n cells takes 5n + 2; comparing every pair goes as n(n − 1)/2."},
 {"u":"39k2.1","q":"What does the machine-change technique say, and what does it hold in trust? Why does n log n beat n² by a factor that grows without end?","a":"If each turn of one machine is done in at most c turns of another, a count that is O(f(n)) on the first is O(f(n)) on the second; held in trust: that every reasonable machine can mimic every other this way; the ratio n²/(n log₂ n) = n/log₂ n passes every bound, so no constant saves the higher family: at a million items, 500 s against 0.02 s","w":["A count on one machine is the same count on every machine; nothing is held in trust; n log n beats n² by a fixed factor of 2","A count O(f(n)) on one machine is O(c · f(n)²) on another; held in trust: the value of c; n log n beats n² only below n = 100","A count O(f(n)) stays O(f(n)); held in trust: that the constant is 1; n² beats n log n for large n"],"why":"What is worth saying about a program is its family."},
 {"u":"39k3.1","q":"What does the faster-machine technique say for each of the three counts? Why does a thousand times the speed buy only about ten more items for a count of 2ⁿ?","a":"On a machine s times faster, in the same time, a count of n fits s times as large a problem, a count of n² fits √s times as large, and a count of 2ⁿ fits a problem only log₂ s items larger; 2^(n₂) = s · 2^(n₁) gives n₂ = n₁ + log₂ s, and log₂ 1000 ≈ 9.97","w":["All three counts fit s times as large a problem; for 2ⁿ a thousand times the speed buys a thousand more items","n fits s times, n² fits s² times, 2ⁿ fits 2ˢ times as large a problem","n fits s times, n² fits √s times, 2ⁿ fits no larger a problem at all, since log₂ s is 0"],"why":"A problem is practical or not by its family, much more than by its machine."},
 {"u":"39k4.1","q":"What does the storage-count technique say, and why can fewer bits not hold every list? What does digital storage buy over a copied tape, and what does it cost?","a":"n items each one of 2ᵇ patterns take nb bits; a row of fewer than nb catches has fewer than 2^(nb) patterns, so two lists would share one; it buys exactness, since each bit is read against a threshold and a copy of a copy has the same bits, misread only at a known rate; it costs the count: every bit must be stored and moved","w":["n items take n + b bits; fewer bits can hold every list if they are packed well; digital storage buys speed and costs exactness","n items take nb bits; fewer cannot hold every list because bits wear out; it buys smaller files and costs nothing","n items take 2^(nb) bits; it buys exactness and costs a little quality at each copy"],"why":"A 12-million-dot photo is 36 million bytes; three minutes of sound about 31.8 million."},
 {"u":"39k5.1","q":"Why is the search not O(1), though it sometimes takes one turn? Why is a copied file exact, though a copied tape is not?","a":"The count is over the worst input of size n, which is 5n + 2; and each bit of a file is read against a threshold, so the copy is exact but for a known misread rate, while a tape loses a little at every copy","w":["The search is O(1), since its best case is one turn; and a copied file loses a little each time, like a tape","The count is over the average input; and a file is exact because it is copied more slowly","The search is O(n²); and neither copy is exact"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39l"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39l"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39l1.1","q":"What does the search-floor technique say? Why are there n + 1 answers and not n?","a":"A search that learns only by yes-or-no questions needs at least log₂(n + 1) of them on some input, and halving needs at most ⌊log₂ n⌋ + 1; the target may be in place 1, 2, …, or n, or nowhere, and “nowhere” is an answer too; m questions give at most 2ᵐ runs, and each answer needs its own","w":["Such a search needs at least n questions; there are n answers, one for each place","Such a search needs at least log₂(n + 1) questions; there are n + 1 answers because the list is counted from 0","Such a search needs at most log₂ n questions on every input; the extra answer is “everywhere”"],"why":"For a million names the floor is 20 and halving takes 20."},
 {"u":"39l2.1","q":"What does the pivot-chance technique say? Why are items i and j compared exactly when one of them is the first pivot chosen between them?","a":"For n different items the random-pivot sort makes on average 2(n + 1)(1 + ½ + ⋯ + 1/n) − 4n comparisons, which grows alike with 2n ln n; until a pivot is chosen among i, …, j the two stay on the same side together; if that first pivot is i or j it is compared with the other, and if it lies strictly between it parts them for good","w":["It makes n(n − 1)/2 comparisons on average; i and j are compared whenever both are pivots","It makes about 2n ln n on average; i and j are compared exactly when a pivot between them is chosen first","It makes n log₂ n on every list; every pair is compared once"],"why":"The chance is 2/(j − i + 1), and the averages add."},
 {"u":"39l3.1","q":"What does the counting-sort technique say? Why does it not contradict the floor of 22l4?","a":"n items, each a whole number below k, are sorted by counting how many there are of each value and writing them out in order, in about 2n + k turns with no comparisons; 22l4’s floor is for sorts that learn only by comparing two items, and this sort looks at the value itself: each look has k possible answers, not two","w":["n items are sorted in about n log₂ n comparisons by counting; it shows the floor of 22l4 was wrong","n items below k are sorted in 2n + k turns; it does not contradict the floor because it only works on sorted lists","n items are sorted in k turns whatever n is; the floor does not apply to numbers"],"why":"It steps outside the floor’s supposition, at the price of k cells."},
 {"u":"39l4.1","q":"Why does counting sort not show the floor of 22l4 is wrong? Why is 2n ln n not the random-pivot sort’s count on every list?","a":"The floor is for sorts that learn only by comparing, and counting sort does not compare; and 2n ln n is the average: the worst case is n(n − 1)/2, when every pivot is the smallest","w":["Counting sort does compare, but fewer times; and 2n ln n is the worst case","The floor is only for lists of names; and the random-pivot sort takes 2n ln n on every list","Counting sort is not a sort; and 2n ln n is the best case"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39m"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39m"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39m1.1","q":"What does the shrink-by-one technique say? Why does the same work per call give n² here and n log n for merge sort?","a":"If T(1) = c and T(n) = T(n − 1) + f(n), then T(n) = c + f(2) + f(3) + ⋯ + f(n); the work at each call is about n in both, but shrinking by one item gives n levels and shrinking by a share gives log₂ n levels","w":["T(n) = c · f(n); merge sort does less work at each call","T(n) = c + f(2) + ⋯ + f(n); merge sort is faster because it makes fewer comparisons per item at each level","T(n) = f(n) + f(n/2) + ⋯; both are n log n"],"why":"The depth of the cutting is the whole difference."},
 {"u":"39m2.1","q":"What does the shares-below-one technique say? Why must α + β be below 1, and what happens to the proof if it is exactly 1?","a":"If T(n) ≤ T(αn) + T(βn) + cn with α + β below 1, then T(n) ≤ Kn with K = c/(1 − α − β); the check gives n(K(α + β) + c), which is at most Kn only when K(1 − α − β) ≥ c; at exactly 1 no K works, the constant would divide by 0, and the true count is n log n","w":["T(n) ≤ Kn for any shares; α + β below 1 only makes K smaller; at exactly 1, K = c","T(n) ≤ Kn with K = c(α + β); the shares must be below 1 so that the pieces are whole numbers; at 1 the count is n²","T(n) ≤ K log n; at α + β = 1 the proof still works with K = 2c"],"why":"With shares ⅕ and 7⁄10 the bound is 10cn: the middle of a list in a linear count."},
 {"u":"39m3.1","q":"What does the guess-and-check technique say? Why is the guess not part of the proof, and the check all of it?","a":"If g meets the rule at the smallest sizes, and the rule applied to g at the smaller sizes gives at most g at n, then T ≤ g at every size; the guess can come from anywhere, a picture of the levels, a run by machine, a hunch, and proves nothing; the induction that the rule keeps the guess is the proof","w":["If g is close to T at a few sizes then T ≤ g; the guess is the proof and the check is a habit","If g meets the rule at the smallest sizes then T = g; no check at larger sizes is needed","A rule can be solved only when the cost of cutting is a power; guessing is not allowed"],"why":"T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n log₂ n comes out as about ½ n log₂² n."},
 {"u":"39m4.1","q":"Why does a check that gives Kn + cn not prove T ≤ Kn? Why is T(n) ≤ 2T(n/2) + cn not linear?","a":"The check must give at most Kn itself, the very bound supposed; Kn + cn is larger; and the shares ½ + ½ add to 1, not below it, so shares-below-one does not apply: the count is n log n","w":["Kn + cn is O(n), so it does prove it; and the rule is linear","The check must give exactly Kn − cn; and the rule is n² because it has two calls","Kn + cn fails only for small n; and the rule is linear once c is small"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39n"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39n"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39n1.1","q":"What does the doubling-array technique say? Why can one put cost a million moves though the average is under three?","a":"n items put one at a time into a list that starts with one cell and doubles whenever it is full cost fewer than 3n moves in all; the put that triggers a copy moves everything the list holds, but copies come at sizes 1, 2, 4, …, which add to less than 2n, and each item is written once","w":["Every put costs at most 3 moves; no put is slow","n puts cost about n² moves in all; one put costs a million because every put copies the list","n puts cost fewer than 3n moves; one put is slow because the list grows by one cell at a time"],"why":"The total is what is small, and the bound holds after every put."},
 {"u":"39n2.1","q":"What does the heap-height technique say? Why does each level of the tree hold twice as many cells as the one above?","a":"A heap of n items has height ⌊log₂ n⌋, so adding an item costs at most that many swaps and taking the largest at most twice that many comparisons; the cells below cell i are 2i and 2i + 1, so level j holds cells 2ʲ to 2ʲ⁺¹ − 1","w":["A heap of n items has height n/2; each level holds one more cell than the one above","A heap has height ⌊log₂ n⌋; each level doubles because every item is stored twice","A heap has height √n; each level holds the square of the one above"],"why":"A million items make 19 levels: at most 19 swaps to add, 38 comparisons to take."},
 {"u":"39n3.1","q":"What does the tree-depth technique say? Why is adding items in random order the same count as the random-pivot sort?","a":"If n different items are added to an empty search tree in random order, the average number of comparisons to add them all is 2(n + 1)Hₙ − 4n; i and j are compared exactly when the first added among i, …, j is i or j, with chance 2/(j − i + 1): the same pairs and chances as in 39l2, the first item added being the top, a pivot","w":["The average is n log₂ n exactly; it is the same count because both sort by merging","The average is 2(n + 1)Hₙ − 4n; it is the same because a search tree is a heap","The average is n(n − 1)/2; it differs from the random-pivot sort by a factor of 2"],"why":"A random order gives a tree only about a quarter deeper than the best."},
 {"u":"39n4.1","q":"What does the load-share technique say? Why do two keys share a slot long before the slots are full?","a":"With n keys in m slots, a look for a key that is there checks on average at most 1 + (n − 1)/m keys, and the chance that some two keys share a slot is 1 − (1 − 1/m)(1 − 2/m)⋯(1 − (n − 1)/m); each new key must miss every slot already used, and those chances multiply: 23 keys in 365 slots share with chance 0.507","w":["A look checks about n keys; two keys share a slot only when n passes m","A look checks at most 1 + (n − 1)/m keys; two keys share early because the scrambling rule is poor","A look checks m/n keys; two keys share a slot with chance n/m"],"why":"A table is fast on average and crowded somewhere almost at once."},
 {"u":"39n5.1","q":"Why is a heap not sorted? Why does a table with more slots than keys still share a slot?","a":"A heap keeps each cell only at least as large as the cells below it, so the bottom row is in no order; and 23 keys in 365 slots already share a slot with chance 0.507","w":["A heap is sorted from left to right in each level; and a table with more slots than keys never shares","A heap is sorted, largest first; and sharing needs more keys than slots","A heap is in random order; and a table shares a slot only when it is half full"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39o"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39o"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39o1.1","q":"What does the remembered-answers technique say? Why do the plain program’s calls grow like F(n) itself?","a":"A recursion whose answer at n needs only answers at smaller sizes can be run with each answer computed once and kept, at a cost of the number of different sizes times the cost of one step; the calls obey C(n) = 1 + C(n − 1) + C(n − 2), which gives C(n) = 2F(n) − 1","w":["Every recursion can be run in one step; the plain program’s calls grow like n²","Each answer is computed once and kept; the plain calls grow like F(n) because each call adds one","Answers cannot be kept, since each call is new; the plain calls grow like n"],"why":"38 additions in place of over two hundred million calls for F(40)."},
 {"u":"39o2.1","q":"What does the alignment-table technique say? Why is a best line-up of the whole made of best line-ups of the shorter pieces?","a":"D(i, j) is the least of D(i − 1, j) + 1, D(i, j − 1) + 1, and D(i − 1, j − 1) plus 0 or 1 as the last letters agree or differ, so the distance is found by filling a table of (n + 1)(m + 1) cells; a line-up ends by a drop, an add or a pair, and what is left lines up shorter pieces: a better line-up of those would make a better whole","w":["D(i, j) is the number of places where the strings differ; the whole is best because each letter is best alone","D(i, j) is the least of three earlier cells plus a cost; the pieces are best because the table is filled from the far corner","D(i, j) = D(i − 1, j − 1) + 1 always; shorter pieces play no part"],"why":"ACGT against AGT has distance 1: drop the C."},
 {"u":"39o3.1","q":"What does the earliest-finish technique say? Why does “greedy never falls behind” prove it, and why does taking the shortest meeting fail?","a":"Taking, again and again, the meeting that ends first among those that fit fits as many meetings into one room as any schedule can; by induction greedy’s r-th meeting ends no later than a best schedule’s r-th, so if the best had one more, it would fit after greedy’s last and greedy would have taken it; a short meeting across two others blocks both","w":["Taking the meeting that starts first fits the most; greedy never falls behind because it starts earliest; the shortest meeting fails because it ends late","Taking the earliest-ending meeting fits the most; it is proved by trying every schedule; the shortest meeting never fails","Taking the longest meeting fits the most; greedy falls behind only once"],"why":"That is the shape every proof that greedy works takes."},
 {"u":"39o4.1","q":"What does the coin-table technique say? Why does greedy fail with coins 1, 3 and 4, and why does the table not?","a":"The fewest coins for an amount v is 1 plus the least, over the coins c no larger than v, of the fewest for v − c, so a table of the amounts 0 to v gives the best answer for any coins; greedy pays 6 as 4 + 1 + 1, committing to the 4, when 3 + 3 is two coins; the table waits until it has seen every smaller amount","w":["The fewest coins is v divided by the largest coin; greedy fails because 4 is even; the table fails too","The fewest coins is 1 plus the fewest for v less the largest coin; greedy and the table agree for coins 1, 3, 4","The fewest coins is found by taking the smallest coin first; greedy fails because it takes too many 1s"],"why":"Greedy is fast when a proof like 39o3’s exists; the table needs no such proof."},
 {"u":"39o5.1","q":"Why is the distance of ACGT and AGT not 3? Why does taking the shortest meeting first not always fit the most?","a":"Lined up from the left they differ in three places, but the distance is the least number of changes, and dropping the C is one; and a short meeting that lies across two others blocks both","w":["The distance counts the places where they differ, which is 3 once the strings are padded; and the shortest meeting always fits the most","The distance is 1 because the strings share three letters; and the shortest meeting fails only when two meetings are equal","The distance is 4, the length of the longer string; and the shortest meeting fails because it ends first"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39p"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39p"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39p1.1","q":"What does the one-row-each technique say? Why is each both 1 on its own row and 0 on every other?","a":"A table is the either of one both for each row where it gives 1, each both taking an input as it is where the row has a 1 and its not where the row has a 0; on its own row every part of the both is 1, and on any other row some input differs, so one part is 0 and the both is 0","w":["A table is the both of one either for each row where it gives 1; each either is 1 on every row but its own","A table is the either of one both for each row where it gives 0; each both is 1 on every row, since it has all the inputs","A table is one both of all the inputs; it is 1 on the rows listed because the inputs are counted, not matched"],"why":"The watering job comes out as five boths of three, right and large."},
 {"u":"39p2.1","q":"What does the neighbour-merge technique say, and which rewrite of 1f gives it? Why are the columns of the grid in the order 00, 01, 11, 10?","a":"Two rows with a 1 that differ in exactly one input merge into one both without that input, and a block of 2ᵏ rows agreeing in all but k inputs merges into one both of the rest; spreading, since x ∧ Y ∨ ¬x ∧ Y = (x ∨ ¬x) ∧ Y = Y; in that order each step across changes one input, so rows that merge sit side by side","w":["Two rows with a 1 that differ in two inputs merge; the double not of 1f; the order 00, 01, 11, 10 counts upward in base two","Any three rows with a 1 merge into one both; spreading; the order does not matter, since every pair of columns merges","Two rows with a 0 merge into one either; the swap of 1f; the order puts the rows with most 1s first"],"why":"The watering job shrinks to f = m ∨ d ¬r."},
 {"u":"39p3.1","q":"What does the two-level-nand technique say? Why does a lone input enter the last gate as its not?","a":"An either of boths is the not-and of the not-ands of the boths; a lone input counts as a both of one, and the not-and of one input is its not","w":["An either of boths is the both of the not-ands of the boths; a lone input enters as it is, since it needs no gate","An either of boths is the not-and of the boths themselves; a lone input enters as its not because every input of a not-and is turned over twice","A both of eithers is the not-and of the eithers; a lone input cannot enter the last gate at all"],"why":"f = nand(¬m, nand(d, ¬r)): four not-ands, sixteen switches."},
 {"u":"39p4.1","q":"What does the circuit-to-table technique say? Why does a row check fail for a circuit of sixty-four inputs, and what takes its place?","a":"A circuit with no loop is checked by running every row, and two such circuits do the same job exactly when they give the same column; a row check costs 2ⁿ runs, and 2⁶⁴ is out of reach; the rewrites of 1f prove sameness by lines, and machines search for a row where two circuits differ","w":["A circuit is checked by running one row; sixty-four inputs need 64 runs, which is too many by hand; nothing takes its place","A circuit with no loop is checked by running every row; the check fails at sixty-four inputs because wide circuits have loops; counting the gates takes its place","Two circuits do the same job when they have the same number of gates; a row check costs n² runs; a table of 64 rows takes its place"],"why":"Same column, same statement (1e14)."},
 {"u":"39p5.1","q":"Why do two rows that differ in two inputs not merge? Why can a circuit with a loop not be checked by its rows alone?","a":"Only rows that differ in one input merge, since spreading takes out one x ∨ ¬x at a time; and a loop may keep a bit (39h), so its output depends on its history and not on the row of inputs alone","w":["They merge into one both without either input; and a loop only slows the circuit, so its rows still give its output","They do not merge because both rows give 0; and a circuit with a loop has no inputs","They merge if they sit side by side in the order 00, 01, 10, 11; and a loop has too many rows to run"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39q"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39q"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39q1.1","q":"What does the nested-loop technique say? Why is the count of three nested loops a count of choices with repeats allowed?","a":"With i from 1 to n and j from 1 to i the inner body runs n(n + 1)/2 times, and with a third loop, l from 1 to j, n(n + 1)(n + 2)/6 times; each run is a choice of l ≤ j ≤ i from n values, where two of them may be the same value","w":["The inner body runs n² times, and n³ with a third loop; each run is a choice of three different values in order","The inner body runs n(n + 1)/2 times, and n(n + 1)(n + 2)/6 with a third loop; each run is a choice of three different values, so no value repeats","The inner body runs 2n times, and 3n with a third loop; the loops are counted by adding their lengths"],"why":"At n = 10 the counts are 55 and 220."},
 {"u":"39q2.1","q":"What does the halving-loop technique say? Why does halving to the whole part run one round fewer than doubling, for the same n?","a":"Halving k to its whole part while k > 1 runs ⌊log₂ k⌋ rounds, and doubling i from 1 while i ≤ n runs ⌊log₂ n⌋ + 1 rounds; the doubling loop runs a round at each of the values 2⁰ to 2ʲ, j + 1 of them, while the halving loop stops on reaching 1 and runs no round there","w":["Both run n/2 rounds; halving runs one fewer because it rounds down","Halving runs ⌊log₂ k⌋ + 1 rounds and doubling ⌊log₂ n⌋; doubling runs fewer because it starts from 1","Halving runs √k rounds and doubling log₂ n; they differ by more than one for large n"],"why":"For 1000: nine rounds of halving, ten of doubling."},
 {"u":"39q3.1","q":"What does the call-tree technique say? Why does a program that calls itself three times on halves land in a higher family than one that calls itself twice?","a":"A program on size n that calls itself on sizes n₁, …, n_a and spends f(n) turns of its own has T(n) = T(n₁) + ⋯ + T(n_a) + f(n); with two calls on halves every level of the tree does n of work, giving n log₂ n, and with three the work grows by r = 3/2 at each level, so the bottom wins: n^(log₂ 3) ≈ n^1.585","w":["T(n) is the number of calls times f(n); three calls give 3n log₂ n, the same family as two","T(n) = T(n₁) + ⋯ + T(n_a) + f(n); three calls on halves give n³, one power for each call","T(n) = T(n₁) × ⋯ × T(n_a); two calls give n² and three give n³"],"why":"22l2 adds the levels of the tree; here its third case applies."},
 {"u":"39q4.1","q":"What does the largest-term technique say? Why should a hand count say from which size on the largest term rules?","a":"A sum of finitely many terms is in the family of its term of highest rank, and the hand count should say the size past which that term is the largest; the family speaks only for large n: in 3n² + 100 n log₂ n + 10⁶ the 3n² passes the middle term from n = 270 and the 10⁶ from n = 578, and below that the start-up rules","w":["A sum is in the family of its first term; the size does not matter, since the family holds for every n","A sum is in the family of the term with the largest number in front, here 10⁶; the size says when the count ends","A sum of terms is in the family of their product; the size is where all the terms are equal"],"why":"Below about 600 items, the 10⁶ start-up rules."},
 {"u":"39q5.1","q":"Why are two nested loops not always n²? Why does doubling from 1 to 1000 run 10 rounds and not log₂ 1000?","a":"What changes the counter decides the family: with i from 1 to n and j doubling up to i the count is about n log₂ n; and the rounds are ⌊log₂ n⌋ + 1, a whole number: 2⁹ = 512 ≤ 1000, so ten rounds, not 9.97","w":["Two nested loops are always n²; and doubling runs 9 rounds, the whole part of 9.97","Two nested loops are 2n, since the lengths add; and doubling runs 500 rounds, half of 1000","Two nested loops are n² unless the inner one is empty; and doubling runs 11 rounds, since 2¹⁰ = 1024"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r39 */
